{"seq": 1, "type": "user_message", "ts": "2026-11-22T17:13:26.758814+00:00", "payload": {"text": "# Your workspace\n\nYou are in `/work`, inside a Linux container with no network access. Write a Go program here.\n[Maelstrom](https://github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom) will start that program once for every node\nof a simulated cluster, send it client requests, and check that the answers are correct.\n\nWhat is installed:\n\n- Go 1.27.\n- Maelstrom 0.2.4, as the `maelstrom` command, so you can test your program yourself.\n- Maelstrom's Go library, already in the module cache. Start your module with:\n\n      go mod init node\n      go get github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/demo/go@v0.0.0-20260710121306-480a8197026d\n\nA node talks to the rest of the cluster through standard input and output: it reads one JSON\nmessage per line on stdin and writes one per line on stdout. Each message has a `src`, a `dest`\nand a `body`, and the body's `type` says what it is. Anything written to stderr is a log line.\nThe library does that framing for you: `maelstrom.NewNode()`, then `Handle` a message type,\n`Reply` to a request, `Send` or `RPC` to another node, and `Run` to start. It also answers the\n`init` message that tells each node its own ID and the IDs of all the nodes.\n\n# Visitor tally\n\nWrite a node for the four turnstiles of a museum, which together keep a running tally of\nvisitors. The tally only ever grows. Clients send each node two kinds of request.\n\n`count` reports that some visitors came in through this turnstile, a non-negative integer of them:\n\n    {\"type\": \"count\", \"msg_id\": 1, \"visitors\": 4}\n    {\"type\": \"counted\", \"in_reply_to\": 1}\n\n`total` asks for the tally so far, over all four turnstiles:\n\n    {\"type\": \"total\", \"msg_id\": 2}\n    {\"type\": \"total_is\", \"in_reply_to\": 2, \"visitors_so_far\": 118}\n\nThe nodes can share a key/value store that Maelstrom runs as a service named `seq-kv`. It is\nsequentially consistent: all nodes see its writes in one order, but a read may be a little out\nof date. In Go, `maelstrom.NewSeqKV(node)` gives you `Read`, `ReadInt`, `Write` and\n`CompareAndSwap`, each taking a `context.Context`. Nodes may also message each other in any\nformat you choose.\n\nFrom time to time the network splits the nodes into groups, and a message sent across a split\nis dropped silently. While that lasts, a `total` may leave out visitors counted elsewhere. At the\nend the network is healed and given time to settle, and then every node's `total` must equal the\nsum of every acknowledged `count`. A `count` that was never answered may be included or not, but\nno visitor may be counted twice.\n\n## How it's graded\n\nYou have 45 minutes and 150 steps, a step being one reply from you, however many tools it runs. If either runs out, you are stopped where you are.\n\nWhen you stop, your program is built with `go build -o node .` and tested 6 times with:\n\n    MAELSTROM_RENAME_BIN=/work/node MAELSTROM_RENAME='{\"fields\": {\"delta\": \"visitors\", \"value\": \"visitors_so_far\"}, \"types\": {\"add\": \"count\", \"add_ok\": \"counted\", \"read\": \"total\", \"read_ok\": \"total_is\"}}' maelstrom test --workload g-counter --bin /usr/local/bin/maelstrom-rename --nemesis partition --node-count 4 --rate 100 --time-limit 20\n\nThe runs are independent, each with its own timing and faults. The attempt passes only if it builds and every run is valid.\n\nMaelstrom itself uses other names for these messages. `maelstrom-rename` stands in for your program, starts it, and translates every message between a client and a node, so test with the command above as it is.\n"}}
