nal design:\n\n```go\npackage main\n\nimport (\n  \"context\"\n  \"sync\"\n  \"time\"\n  maelstrom \"github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/demo/go\"\n)\n\ntype node struct {\n  n *maelstrom.Node\n  kv *maelstrom.KV\n\n  mu sync.Mutex\n  local int64          // total visitors counted at this node\n  flushed int64        // highest value successfully written to kv\n  known map[string]int64 // highest known value per node id (peers), cache\n}\n\nfunc main() {\n  nd := maelstrom.NewNode()\n  s := &node{n: nd, kv: maelstrom.NewSeqKV(nd), known: map[string]int64{}}\n  nd.Handle(\"count\", s.handleCount)\n  nd.Handle(\"total\", s.handleTotal)\n  nd.Handle(\"peer_read\", s.handlePeerRead)\n  go s.flusher()\n  if err := nd.Run(); err != nil { log.Fatal(err) }\n}\n```\n\nhandleCount:\n```go\nvar body struct{ Visitors int `json:\"visitors\"` } // careful: the field name after rename is \"visitors\"\njson.Unmarshal(msg.Body, &body)\nif body.Visitors < 0 { body.Visitors = 0 }\nmu.Lock(); local += int64(body.Visitors); mu.Unlock()\nsignal flush (non-blocking send on chan)\nreturn s.n.Reply(msg, map[string]any{\"type\":\"counted\"})\n```\n\nWait — the reply type. The rename proxy maps `add_ok` → `counted`. So we must reply with `{\"type\":\"counted\"}`? Hmm. Careful! The rename proxy translates messages between client and node, in both directions. Which direction is translated? \"maelstrom-rename stands in for your program, starts it, and translates every message between a client and a node\". The fields map: `{\"delta\": \"visitors\", \"value\": \"visitors_so_far\"}` and types `{\"add\": \"count\", \"add_ok\": \"counted\", \"read\": \"total\", \"read_ok\": \"total_is\"}`.\n\nSo when the client sends `add` with `delta` to the node, the node sees `count` with `visitors`. When the node replies `counted`, the client sees `add_ok`. So the mapping is symmetric: the type name `count` ↔ `add`; the node's `counted` ↔ client's `add_ok`. So yes, we emit `counted` and clients see `add_ok`. Similarly `total` → `read`, `total_is` with `visitors_so_far` → `read_ok` with `value`.\n\nGood, matches the task description.\n\nhandleTotal:\n```go\ngo func(){\n  total := s.gather()\n  s.n.Reply(msg, map[string]any{\"type\":\"total_is\",\"visitors_so_far\": total})\n}()\nreturn nil\n```\nActually handle synchronously (it's already in a goroutine).\n\ngather:\n- own := local\n- for each peer: value := max(cached, kvRead(peer), peerQuery(peer))\n- sum.\n\nDo kv reads and peer queries in parallel with a deadline.\n\nhandlePeerRead: reply `{\"type\":\"peer_read_ok\",\"total\": local}`.\n\nk/v key names: use \"counter/\" + nodeID.\n\nFlusher: a goroutine with a channel `dirty chan struct{}` (buffered 1) and retry loop:\n\n```go\nfunc (s *node) flusher() {\n  for {\n    <-s.dirty\n    for {\n      mu.Lock(); v := s.local; f := s.flushed; mu.Unlock()\n      if v <= f { break }\n      ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 2*time.Second)\n      err := s.kv.Write(ctx, s.key(), v)\n      cancel()\n      if err == nil {\n         mu.Lock(); if v > s.flushed { s.flushed = v }; mu.Unlock()\n         // loop again in case local advanced\n         continue\n      }\n      time.Sleep(50 * time.Millisecond)\n      // retry until success\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWait: retry until success — but if it fails, the inner loop spins forever until success; that's what we want (kv always reachable though). But careful: the outer loop takes a `<-s.dirty`; if we're stuck in the inner retry loop and more counts arrive, the dirty channel holds 1 signal; after success we break out of the inner loop only when v <= f, then read dirty again (which may be buffered) → good.\n\nThere's a subtle race: v is read, then while writing, local increases → after success, loop continues and writes the new value. Then break when equal.\n\nBut careful: since kv is always reachable, writes succeed in ~1ms.\n\nAlso to guarantee the write ordering (monotone), only this goroutine writes → fine.\n\nHmm, one