\nNow, is there a problem with `local` being int64 and visitors potentially large? Sum over 2000 ops with delta 0..9 → tiny.\n\nAnother critical detail: the `count` message field. The client sends `{\"type\":\"add\",\"msg_id\":N,\"delta\":X}` → rename → `{\"type\":\"count\",\"msg_id\":N,\"visitors\":X}`. We must parse `visitors`.\n\nWait, careful: does the rename proxy also rename the msg_id field? No, just the listed fields and types.\n\nAnd our reply: `{\"type\":\"counted\",\"in_reply_to\":N}` → proxy → `{\"type\":\"add_ok\",\"in_reply_to\":N}`. The client's schema check requires `add_ok` with `in_reply_to`. Good.\n\nHmm — one risk: what if the rename proxy only translates in one direction (client→node) and expects the node to speak the *original* names? Let's just test it. I'll write the program and run the test.\n\nAlso `total`: our reply `{\"type\":\"total_is\",\"in_reply_to\":N,\"visitors_so_far\":T}`.\n\nNow let's also double check that the peer messages won't get translated or break the proxy: the proxy forwards node→node messages untouched? Our peer messages have src = our node id, dest = peer node id, type \"peer_read\" — not in the rename map → passthrough. There is a risk the proxy crashes on unknown types. Test will show.\n\nAlternative to peer messaging: avoid it entirely and rely on kv with multiple reads. Hmm. But let's try peer messaging first; if the proxy misbehaves, fall back.\n\nActually — wait. There's an important consideration I should double check: `--bin /usr/local/bin/maelstrom-rename`. Maelstrom launches this binary for each node with args? Normally Maelstrom runs the bin with command-line args and communicates via stdin/stdout. The rename proxy reads MAELSTROM_RENAME_BIN and spawns /work/node. Fine.\n\nLet's now check what `init` looks like through the proxy — the node needs `node_ids`. Presumably forwarded.\n\nLet me write the code.\n\nOne more design consideration: what if `total` arrives before `init`? No.\n\nWhat about the reply to `total` containing int64 → JSON number. Fine.\n\nLet me write it.\n\nAdditional robustness: also handle `count` requests with a stale duplicate msg_id? Not needed.\n\nLet me write the code now.\n\n```go\npackage main\n\nimport (\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"log\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"sync\"\n\t\"time\"\n\n\tmaelstrom \"github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/demo/go\"\n)\n\nconst (\n\tpeerTimeout   = 400 * time.Millisecond\n\tgatherBudget  = 1200 * time.Millisecond\n\tflushTimeout  = 2 * time.Second\n\tkeyPrefix     = \"counter/\"\n)\n\ntype server struct {\n\tn  *maelstrom.Node\n\tkv *maelstrom.KV\n\tdirty chan struct{}\n\n\tmu     sync.Mutex\n\tlocal  int64\n\tflushed int64\n\tknown  map[string]int64\n}\n```\n\ngather with retries:\n\n```go\nfunc (s *server) gather() int64 {\n\tids := s.n.NodeIDs()\n\tme := s.n.ID()\n\n\tdeadline := time.Now().Add(gatherBudget)\n\tfor {\n\t\ttype res struct{ kv, peer int64; ok bool }\n\t\tresults := make(map[string]*res, len(ids))\n\t\tvar mu sync.Mutex\n\t\tvar wg sync.WaitGroup\n\t\tfor _, id := range ids {\n\t\t\tif id == me { continue }\n\t\t\twg.Add(1)\n\t\t\tgo func(id string) {\n\t\t\t\tdefer wg.Done()\n\t\t\t\tctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), peerTimeout)\n\t\t\t\tdefer cancel()\n\t\t\t\tr := &res{}\n\t\t\t\tif v, err := s.kv.ReadInt(ctx, keyPrefix+id); err == nil {\n\t\t\t\t\tr.kv = int64(v)\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\tif v, ok := s.peerTotal(ctx, id); ok {\n\t\t\t\t\tr.peer = v; r.ok = true\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\tmu.Lock(); results[id] = r; mu.Unlock()\n\t\t\t}(id)\n\t\t}\n\t\twg.Wait()\n\n\t\tallOK := true\n\t\ts.mu.Lock()\n\t\tfor id, r := range results {\n\t\t\tv := r.kv\n\t\t\tif r.peer > v { v = r.peer }\n\t\t\tif v > s.known[id] { s.known[id] = v }\n\t\t\tif !r.ok { allOK = false }\n\t\t}\n\t\ttotal := s.local\n\t\tfor _, v := range s.known { total += v }\n\t\ts.mu.Unlock()\n\t\tif allOK || time.Now().After(deadline) { return total }\n\t\t// retry\n\t}\n}\n```\n\nHmm, careful: `s.known` may contain entries