{"seq": 87, "type": "assistant_message", "ts": "2026-11-22T17:19:22.019217+00:00", "payload": {"reasoning": "Done. 6 test runs (4 exact-config + 2 stress) all valid.\n\nWrite the summary.", "text": "Program is written, built, and verified. Summary of what I built in `/work` (`main.go`, `go.mod`, `go.sum`):\n\n**Design** — each of the four turnstiles owns exactly one value: the number of visitors that came through it.\n- On `count`: add `visitors` to the node's in-memory tally and immediately reply `counted`; a background flusher mirrors the (non-decreasing) tally into `seq-kv` under key `counter/<node-id>`, retrying until the write sticks. Only one goroutine ever writes a node's key, so the store value never goes backwards. An acknowledged count therefore can never be lost.\n- On `total`: for each other node take `max(seq-kv read, direct peer query)`. Both are always lower bounds on that node's true tally, so the max never double counts; it is exact whenever the direct query succeeds (always the case once the network heals). Values are also cached per peer so a node's own answers never decrease. If some peer query fails (split in progress) the gather is retried within a short budget, then answered with the best partial knowledge — allowed while partitioned.\n- Peer protocol is `peer_read` / `peer_read_ok {total}`; `seq-kv` is a backstop for when peers are unreachable.\n\n**Verification** — built with `go build -o node .` and ran the exact grading command 4 times (all `valid?`, e.g. final reads `(1100 1100 1100 1100)`, acceptable `([1100 1100])`, 0 info ops), plus 2 extra stress runs with `--nemesis-interval 3` to force far more partitions — every run passed with all four nodes' final reads exactly matching the sum of acknowledged counts.", "tool_calls": [], "usage": {"input_tokens": 67296, "output_tokens": 415}}}
